Express is a package for writing HTTP routes in Node.js, which runs JavaScript outside the browser. Save this complete separate example as server.mjs in a local project with the supplied Express dependency installed:
```javascript
import express from 'express';
const app = express();
app.use(express.json({ limit: '20kb' }));
const visits = [];
app.get('/visits', (req, res) => res.json(visits));
app.post('/visits', (req, res) => {
  const minutes = req.body?.minutes;
  if (!Number.isInteger(minutes) || minutes < 1 || minutes > 180) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Use 1 to 180 whole minutes' });
  }
  const visit = { id: crypto.randomUUID(), minutes };
  visits.push(visit);
  res.status(201).json(visit);
});
app.listen(3000, '127.0.0.1', () => console.log('Local server ready'));
```
Run with Node.js 22.13 or newer. The callback receives req for request information and res for the response. listen starts the local server on port 3000.

