Here, express() takes no arguments and returns the application object used to register routes. crypto.randomUUID() also takes no arguments; each call returns a UUID string for an identifier, not a numeric sequence. The example stores that returned string in visit.id. These calls do not themselves verify the caller or save data durably.

Set membership helps compare ids. Suppose saved ids are {x, y} and requested ids are {x, z}. Their overlap is {x}, because only x occurs in both. z is requested but missing, and y is stored but not requested. Positions do not decide membership. A JavaScript Set stores distinct values and has(id) checks whether a particular id is present.

```javascript
const savedIds = new Set(["x", "y"]);
console.log(savedIds.has("x"));
console.log(savedIds.has("z"));
```
This prints true, then false. has receives one candidate value, returns a Boolean and does not change the set.

An Express middleware function typically receives req, res, and next. It either sends an error response and returns, or calls next() to continue to the route. Validation middleware can reject a malformed body before writing. A final error handler can report a useful failure without exposing private records, passwords, or internal credentials. Responses and logs must not reveal another user's data just to explain permission denial.

A minimal continuation callback example is:
```javascript
function requireSession(req, res, next) {
  if (!req.session) return res.status(401).json({error: "Sign in"});
  next();
}
```
Express supplies next; calling it without an argument continues to later middleware. The missing-session branch returns before that call. This fragment requires an Express route and verified-session middleware; it is not a sign-in implementation.

Calling requireSession({session:{}}, {}, () => console.log("Continue")) prints Continue through the supplied callback. This local call demonstrates continuation only; the verified-session fixture is not authentication.

The supplied parsePage helper accepts digit-only text, converts it with Number, and checks Number.isSafeInteger.

```javascript
console.log(Number("12"));
console.log(Number.isSafeInteger(12));
console.log(Number.isSafeInteger(1.5));
console.log(Number.isSafeInteger(9007199254740992));
```
The outputs are 12, true, false, false. Number converts the supplied text to a number. Number.isSafeInteger receives a value and returns whether it is an integer within JavaScript’s safely representable integer range. It does not convert quoted text or validate the allowed page range; those remain separate rules.

