This exercise controls a Promise manually so you can inspect waiting without relying on network timing. new Promise(resolve => { ... }) immediately provides a resolve function; finishLoading saves a callback that resolves it with { default: Report }. The Report code is already present: this simulates a pending module and does not split or download a real file.

This smaller Promise example exposes the callback and its effect:
```javascript
let finish;
const pending = new Promise(resolve => { finish = resolve; });
pending.then(value => console.log(value));
finish("Ready");
```
The constructor takes an executor callback and returns a pending Promise. It immediately gives the callback a resolve function, stored here as finish. Calling finish supplies the eventual result, so the reaction later prints Ready. No network request or downloaded module is involved.

Task represents asynchronous work. Task { ... } starts a task with a closure containing its instructions. A stored Task<Void, Never>? means an optional task whose successful result is Void, no useful returned value, and whose exposed failure type is Never, meaning it does not throw an error out to its caller. The closure can still perform throwing work if it handles the error internally. task?.cancel() uses optional chaining: call cancel only if a task exists. Cancellation is cooperative. It signals that the work should stop; code must check or reach a cancellation-aware operation. Task.checkCancellation() throws if cancellation has been requested, and Task.isCancelled reads that state as a Bool.

Task.sleep suspends the current task for the requested duration without promising an exact resume time; cancellation can make it throw. This complete top-level async fragment makes a task and observes cancellation:
```swift
let work = Task {
    try await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(10))
    return "Finished"
}
work.cancel()
do { print(try await work.value) }
catch is CancellationError { print("Cancelled") }
```
Task receives the work closure and returns a task handle. cancel requests cooperative cancellation, not a successful work result. Awaiting value observes the result or error; this example prints Cancelled rather than waiting ten seconds.

Overloading means functions can share a name when their input signatures differ. loadEntries(_ data: Data) loads supplied bytes; loadEntries(from url: URL) reads the file and then reuses the byte decoder. The underscore lets the Data call omit a label, while from is the URL call's label. Data("broken".utf8) makes bytes from the letters of broken using UTF-8 text encoding. Those bytes are not valid JSON for [PracticeEntry], so they should exercise the catch path. A file-read failure and a decode failure are different causes, but both need an understandable outcome.

This small overload pair uses the same two input shapes without hiding the file read:
```swift
import Foundation
func loadLabels(_ bytes: Data) throws -> [String] {
    try JSONDecoder().decode([String].self, from: bytes)
}
func loadLabels(from url: URL) throws -> [String] {
    try loadLabels(Data(contentsOf: url))
}
print(try loadLabels(Data("[\"Map\"]".utf8)))
```
It prints ["Map"]. The first function accepts bytes and returns decoded strings or throws. The labeled overload accepts a file URL, reads bytes, and delegates to the first. To exercise it, use the earlier temporary-file write pattern with these JSON bytes and call try loadLabels(from: url); a missing file throws. The local companion test must verify both forms; this text does not claim browser file access.

